  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nA beautiful midsummer day was in progress across SW KS at\nmidday, with satellite imagery depicting a perfectly clear sky\nand surface observations showing light winds. Temperatures\nSaturday afternoon will represent a relative minimum, with\nreadings actually a few degrees below early August normals in\nthe lower 90s. A few locations may fall short of 90 degrees this\nafternoon as weak cold advection continues on weak northeast\nwinds. \n\nClear, calm, and quiet tonight as winds go light and variable.\nA clear sky, light/calm winds and relatively dry air will once\nagain allow for strong radiational cooling tonight, at least\nfor midsummer standards. Sunrise Sunday temperatures will be\nseveral degrees below normal again, in the 60-65 range. \n\nAll models show a strong heating trend Sunday and Monday, with\nthickness and 850 mb temperatures climbing quickly through the\nperiod. 850 mb temperatures as high as 33C will support\ntemperatures well over 100 Monday afternoon. Once again, NBM \ndoes not appear hot enough for the next few days, and increased\nSunday and Monday max temperature grids several degrees. \n12z MAV guidance is forecasting Monday afternoon temperatures of\n105-108 across much of SW KS, which will be supported by\nimproved mixing and elevated south winds. NBM probability of\nmaximum temperatures exceeding 100 degrees is highest, >90%,\nacross the southwest zones where confidence is highest the\nhottest temperatures will occur Monday afternoon. The current\ngrids support apparent temperatures/heat index <105 for all\nzones Monday, so a heat advisory does not currently look\nrequired. \n\nA completely dry, very quiet regime, typical of early August,\nwill persist through at least 7 pm Monday, with all pops at 0%\nand few if any clouds. A few thunderstorms may enter the\nnorthwest zones Monday night, as a weak shortwave rounds the\nnortheast periphery of the subtropical ridge. This subtropical\nmidlevel ridge, near 598-600 dm in intensity, will move little\nover the coming week, slowly crawling north into the Great\nBasin, with SW KS beneath the eastern arm of the strongest\nsubsidence. Tuesday through Friday, a stray storm may sneak into\nthe northwest zones amid the weak NWly flow aloft. NBM pops are\ncorrectly minimal, and confident most locations will remain dry\nthrough the upcoming week. High confidence of a hot and dry\npattern persisting through August 10, with CPC's 6-10 day\noutlook showcasing an 80% probability of above normal\ntemperatures, and below normal precipitation.",
